---
---

<section id="motivation" class="py-16 sm:py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight uppercase leading-none">
      All the benefits of Protobuf.
      <br />
      <span class="font-serif italic font-normal normal-case text-[#FF4F18]">
        Faster, easier, and simpler.
      </span>
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg sm:text-xl text-ink/80 font-medium">
      You shouldn’t have to abandon <code>.proto</code> or accept bloated zero-copy pointer graphs
      just to get fast serialization and readable packets.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- 4 Full-Width Rows Hitting the Core Tones -->
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <!-- 1. FASTER -->
    <div class="os-window overflow-hidden">
      <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
        <span class="font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold uppercase">1. Faster</span>
        <div class="os-stripes"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-[#FAF8F2] grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 items-center">
        <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-2.5">
          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase">
            No byte-by-byte varint loops. No two-pass encoding.
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
            Protobuf’s{' '}
            <a
              href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LEB128"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="doc-link"
            >
              LEB128
            </a>{' '}
            varints check a continuation bit on every single byte, stalling CPUs with branch
            mispredictions, and force encoders to walk the message tree twice just to calculate
            nested lengths. OmniBuf encodes integer byte-length in the first byte (
            <a
              href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Find_first_set"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              class="doc-link"
            >
              <code>TZCNT</code>
            </a>
            ) for single-load decoding and writes messages forward in a single pass.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lg:col-span-4">
          <div class="p-4 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
            <div class="text-acid font-bold">Over 6x faster encode</div>
            <div class="text-white font-bold">Over 8x faster decode</div>
            <div class="text-paper/75 text-sm">1-pass write &bull; 1-load varints</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. EASIER TO USE -->
    <div class="os-window overflow-hidden">
      <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
        <span class="font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold uppercase">2. Easier to Use</span>
        <div class="os-stripes"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-[#FAF8F2] grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 items-center">
        <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-2.5">
          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase">
            Debug packets without a schema &amp; use normal structs.
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
            Protobuf made a mistake by lumping strings, bytes, sub-messages, maps, and packed arrays
            into a single opaque wire type (<code>Wire Type 2</code>). OmniBuf spends slightly more
            tag space (~5% message size) so wire types match Protobuf types directly. Any CLI,
            proxy, or log viewer can decode values without the original <code>.proto</code> file,
            and you never have to wrestle with{' '}
            <a href="https://flatbuffers.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="doc-link">
              FlatBuffers
            </a>
            ’ bottom-up builders.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lg:col-span-4">
          <div class="p-4 bg-white border-2 border-ink rounded font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-ink">Schema-less value inspection</div>
            <div class="text-ink/80 text-sm">
              + Optional Field #0 inline field names for instant named JSON
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. SIMPLER TO IMPLEMENT -->
    <div class="os-window overflow-hidden">
      <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#E5E0D5]">
        <span class="font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold uppercase">
          3. Simpler to Implement
        </span>
        <div class="os-stripes"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 bg-[#FAF8F2] grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 items-center">
        <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-2.5">
          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase">
            A flat [Tag][Value] stream with no vtables or pointer graphs.
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
            Zero-copy formats require complex vtables, relative pointer graphs, alignment padding,
            and arena allocators. Even Protobuf requires special cases for groups, unpacked vs.
            packed arrays, and synthetic <code>MapEntry</code> sub-messages. OmniBuf is a flat
            stream of <code>[Tag][Value]</code> pairs with 4 integer widths (<code>1, 2, 4, 9</code>{' '}
            bytes) and 16 explicit wire types.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lg:col-span-4">
          <div class="p-4 bg-white border-2 border-ink rounded font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-ink">No vtables or pointer graphs</div>
            <div class="text-ink/80 text-sm">
              4 integer sizes &bull; 16 wire types &bull; Flat stream layout
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. ALL THE BENEFITS OF PROTOBUF -->
    <div class="os-window bg-ink text-paper ring-4 ring-acid overflow-hidden">
      <div class="os-titlebar bg-[#1F1F1F] border-b-2 border-ink text-paper">
        <span class="font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold uppercase text-acid">
          4. All the Benefits of Protobuf
        </span>
        <div
          class="os-stripes"
          style="background: repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom, #FAF8F2, #FAF8F2 2px, transparent 2px, transparent 4px);"
        ></div>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 items-center">
        <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-2.5">
          <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold uppercase text-white">
            Keep your .proto files, schema evolution, and compact payloads.
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-paper/90 leading-relaxed">
            OmniBuf works directly with your existing <code>.proto</code> schemas and generated
            structs. You keep field numbers, safe forward and backward compatibility, near-Protobuf
            wire size (40% smaller than FlatBuffers), and raw byte concatenation (
            <code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) for zero-parse message merging.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lg:col-span-4">
          <div class="p-4 bg-acid text-ink border-2 border-ink rounded font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold space-y-1">
            <div>✓ Existing .proto schemas</div>
            <div>✓ Safe field evolution</div>
            <div>✓ append(msgA, msgB...) merge</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
